# USB access for harbour-pipecam.
#
# WHY THIS FILE IS NEEDED
# The endoscope camera is a vendor-class device (interface class 0xff, subclass
# 0xf0), so the kernel's uvcvideo driver never binds it and no /dev/videoN is
# created. The app drives the USB endpoints directly with libusb, which means it
# needs to open the raw device node under /dev/bus/usb/. Those nodes default to
# root:usb 0660 and the Sailfish app user is not in the `usb` group, so without
# this rule the camera is visible on the bus but cannot be opened.
#
# SCOPE
# Matched on the two known VID:PIDs of this camera family and on the device node
# only (DEVTYPE=="usb_device"), not on its interfaces. Nothing else on the USB
# bus is affected.
#
# WHY MODE=0666 AND NOT TAG+="uaccess"
# uaccess hands the node to whoever is logged in at the seat, but logind then
# re-applies its own ACL and resets the node whenever the session state changes
# — which on a phone happens every time the screen locks. A plain mode makes the
# camera work without a re-login and without adding the app user to a group.
# The device is a camera with no stored data and no writable firmware, so a
# permissive node costs nothing.
#
# WHY THE FILENAME STARTS WITH 999- AND NOT 99-
# This is the whole reason the rule works, so do not "tidy" the name.
#
# Sailfish on Android-based hardware ships /lib/udev/rules.d/999-android-system.rules,
# which contains a catch-all at line 107:
#
#     SUBSYSTEM=="usb", KERNEL=="*", MODE="0660", GROUP="usb", OWNER="root"
#
# udev applies rule files in lexical filename order and the LAST assignment
# wins. "99-harbour-pipecam-usb.rules" sorts BEFORE "999-android-system.rules"
# ('-' is 0x2D, '9' is 0x39), so a 99- prefixed file is silently overwritten by
# that catch-all and the node stays root:usb 0660 — udevadm test even shows our
# MODE 0666 being applied and then replaced.
#
# "999-harbour-pipecam-usb.rules" sorts after "999-android-system.rules"
# ('a' < 'h') and therefore wins. The device's own 999-veroval.rules solves the
# same problem the same way.
#
# Diagnose with:
#     udevadm test /sys/bus/usb/devices/<n-n.n> 2>&1 | grep -E 'MODE|GROUP'
# The last MODE line is the one that takes effect.
#
# Same overall approach as /etc/udev/rules.d/99-flipper.rules (harbour-sflipper)
# and 99-harbour-idrone-catsniffer.rules (harbour-idrone) — those match on
# SUBSYSTEM=="tty", which the Android catch-all does not touch, which is why a
# 99- prefix is enough for them and not for us.

# "Geek szitman / supercamera" — the common USeePlus endoscope
SUBSYSTEM=="usb", ENV{DEVTYPE}=="usb_device", ATTR{idVendor}=="2ce3", ATTR{idProduct}=="3828", MODE="0666"
# Same hardware, alternate ID pair used by some batches
SUBSYSTEM=="usb", ENV{DEVTYPE}=="usb_device", ATTR{idVendor}=="0329", ATTR{idProduct}=="2022", MODE="0666"
